<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elagabalus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 204–222</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Heliogabalus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elagabalus was Roman emperor from 218 to 222, born Varius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a> Bassianus into the priestly family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a> and great-nephew of the empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julia-domna">Julia Domna</a>. Raised to power as a teenager by his grandmother Julia Maesa, who claimed he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a>'s son, he made his god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabal">Elagabal</a> the chief deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His religious innovations and conduct caused scandal, and the Praetorian Guard murdered him and his mother in 222.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elagabalus.jpg" alt="Elagabalus" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Roman_emperor_Elagabalus,_ca._221_AD,_Capitoline_Museums_(20814003112).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
